#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-3,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,0,5 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 1,2,3 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1)j+(-1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-10i+8j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-10,8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-10)+(-3)\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,2,-1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,-1,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot (2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\cdot (0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))+(-1)\\cdot (0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =20-2-2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105595 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "483b758a-9b14-5a72-87e5-70f27a0acf48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4349" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $2\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times 3\\mathbf{c}) = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (b\\times 3c) & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times 3c)] & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =5 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a) & =-\\frac{5}{6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0b4d33-7699-5068-90f1-663534cd662d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Scalar Triple Products Using Its Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29595116-806d-5a50-ab1b-9695013bab97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410234} {:answer/id #uuid "6cddf496-550e-503d-97cf-86f4c416cb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410274} {:answer/id #uuid "87da3e47-9864-54d6-b40d-2913c6ae3373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410286} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4d127a-493d-5de0-a877-1ac772d55fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410329} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d42b31-2f13-5fd5-a529-f48c87f1b72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cddf496-550e-503d-97cf-86f4c416cb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410274} :answer-field/id #uuid "81bc68bf-9d8f-562a-bde6-27ff85778ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35202296-3c02-529f-93c9-ab75d2fe58b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-128355" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b),$ if $b\\times c=⟨4,3,-5⟩$ and $a=⟨-2,8,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,8,2⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4+8\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-8+24-10 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1868bb81-71b6-5f50-8657-a4862c1f609e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285005} {:answer/id #uuid "2cdccd16-86aa-576c-bb76-82d6055e2720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285184} {:answer/id #uuid "39d88586-7aa2-5d90-adfe-ac64bd3a8ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285331} {:answer/id #uuid "a567177a-6805-5d8a-9d10-fde433c3ecd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286298} {:answer/id #uuid "de641599-4761-523f-91dd-a114e0a85611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1868bb81-71b6-5f50-8657-a4862c1f609e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285005} :answer-field/id #uuid "699666f7-e0ff-5c75-8d34-1ee5373ce603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75f9d3bd-a296-5c60-a85d-8a17773b59f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20050" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,3,2⟩$, $b=⟨3,4,1⟩$ and $c=⟨2,0,1⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)-3(3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =4-3-16 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ba6b6b5-ac1d-5a3a-876a-af51fdf50e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6351ad9-60eb-5c1f-b085-0679828a8f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102663} {:answer/id #uuid "bd21b2e9-ea49-5df3-8d99-5c5fe08c5507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102664} {:answer/id #uuid "de9107a1-a714-5163-bbc2-967ba2fe6b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102665} {:answer/id #uuid "f05b096d-b558-5756-8dfd-2ddf5e8baaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102666} {:answer/id #uuid "f58ada1e-09a7-5c98-bb8c-566d28d2cc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102667}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f7550-d466-5039-802c-5c3f0c6aa926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102662}] :question/id #uuid "cb9b799b-95c5-5b8c-b1f0-b4bcc39f76c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-20065" :question/problem "Given that $a = ⟨0,-2, 3⟩$, $b = ⟨4,-2, 1⟩$, and $c = ⟨3,-1, 2⟩$, find $a \\cdot (b \\cdot c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3276117f-c9fb-5648-baa7-3ddf01111aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3276117f-c9fb-5648-baa7-3ddf01111aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408047} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c0686c8-e24f-53f9-b174-994346511b51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40346cc1-7283-5552-9863-224be650a17a" :question/math-academy-id "q-275350" :question/problem "Let $r=⟨-4,-1,2⟩$, $q=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $p=⟨5,-2,-1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot (q\\times p)=$\n\n$-21$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)(3\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =(-4)(-3+2)+(2-5)+2(4-15) \\\\  & =4-3-22 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aadbe221-860c-5e54-a0de-bd7fb0bef36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aadbe221-860c-5e54-a0de-bd7fb0bef36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419781} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea71914-4f17-5f64-8016-140e481b698d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20209d26-6af3-5176-8409-17df2aefc0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-317313" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,-3,0⟩$, $b=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $c=⟨2,0,-4⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(2\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 0)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,((-2)\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot 2)\\,+\\,0\\,((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1\\,(-8)\\,-\\,(-3)\\,(8)\\,+\\,0\\,(-4) \\\\  & =-8+24+0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299587 